1. Optimized Linux File System Layout for a 50 GB Server
Answer:
 *The given server has only 50 GB of storage, but it has to handle system logs, user files, and application data.
 *The main problem is that log files can keep increasing continuously. 
 *If they occupy the complete disk, the operating system may not have enough space for normal operations, and important services may stop working.
 
 *To avoid this situation, I would divide the disk into different partitions instead of keeping everything under one partition. This makes storage management easier and also prevents one type of data from consuming all the available space.

A suitable layout for the 50 GB disk would be:
   */boot – 1 GB
   *swap – 4 GB
   */ – 12 GB
   */var – 10 GB
   */home – 12 GB
   */data – 11 GB

 *The /var partition is especially important because Linux stores log files and other frequently changing data under /var. 
  By keeping it separate, even if the logs grow, they will not directly fill the root filesystem.

 *For the log files, I would use logrotate. It automatically rotates old logs, compresses them, and removes very old files according to the configured policy. 
  This prevents a single log file from continuously increasing in size.

 *I would also monitor the disk regularly. If the /var partition starts approaching its limit, the administrator can receive an alert and take action before the disk becomes full.

 *The important constraint in this problem is that log files must not exceed 10 GB. 
  Therefore, the log rotation policy should be configured with a suitable size limit and number of backup files. Old logs can be compressed so that they consume less space.

 *This design also helps maintain system stability.
  For example, if user files suddenly increase, they will mainly affect /home instead of consuming the space required by the operating system. Similarly, growing logs will be restricted to /var.

 *Therefore, using separate partitions, log rotation, compression, and disk monitoring provides better utilization of the 50 GB storage and reduces the chance of critical services stopping because of disk exhaustion.

Linux Commands / Code:
1.Check disk usage: df -h 
2.Check which directories are using more space: du -sh /var/*
  Example logrotate configuration:
    /var/log/myapp.log {
    daily
    rotate 7
    size 1G
    compress
    delaycompress
    missingok
    notifempty
}
3.Test logrotate configuration: sudo logrotate -d /etc/logrotate.conf
4.Force log rotation for testing: sudo logrotate -f /etc/logrotate.conf
5.Simple disk-space monitoring script:
  #!/bin/bash
  USAGE=$(df /var | awk 'NR==2 {print $5}' | tr -d '%')
  if [ "$USAGE" -ge 80 ]; then
    echo "Warning: /var is more than 80% full"
  else
    echo "/var storage is under control"
  fi

